course: dictionary
lesson: 1444
title: Cartilage: bendy but strong
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words name parts of the body that you cannot see every day. Cartilage is one of them. Read the clues around it: «The kneecap protects the front of the knee» and «it is soft cartilage that turns to bone over the first years». Cartilage is soft, and it turns into bone, so it must be a material that is softer than bone.
explain: Context also tells you where cartilage is. «Spotted eagle rays ... have skeletons of cartilage (the bendy stuff in your ear), not bone.» The brackets give a clue: you can bend the stuff in your ear, so cartilage is bendy. A good check is to swap your meaning into the sentence and see if it still makes sense.
explain: Words can have more than one meaning. Cartilage can mean the material itself, or a piece of it, as in a torn cartilage in the knee. Look at the words around it to see which meaning the writer chose.
fact: Cartilage is a strong, bendy material in the body, softer than bone.
fact: Cartilage is found in your ears, at the end of your nose and between bones in your joints.
fact: «Cartilage» can also mean a piece of this material, especially in the knee: a torn cartilage.
fact: Babies have more cartilage, and it hardens into bone as they grow.
fact: Sharks and rays have skeletons made of cartilage, not bone.
exercise: «The kneecap protects the front of the knee; in babies it is soft cartilage that turns to bone over the first years.» What is cartilage? | A kind of muscle that pulls the leg | A thin layer of skin on the knee | *A strong, bendy material that is softer than bone | A hard white bone that never bends
exercise: «Spotted eagle rays ... have skeletons of cartilage (the bendy stuff in your ear), not bone.» What is the bendy stuff in your ear? | muscle | skin | *cartilage | bone
exercise: Where can you find cartilage in your body? | your fingernails | your eyebrows | *your ears | your hair
test: Cartilage covers the ends of bones at joints. Why does this help? | It stops you feeling hungry | It gives the bones their colour | It makes the bones heavier | *It lets the bones move smoothly without rubbing
test: «Babies have more cartilage, which hardens into bone as they grow.» What happens to cartilage as babies grow? | It turns into skin | *It hardens into bone | A shell grows over it | It melts away
exam: «A torn cartilage in the knee» means what? | a bone that is broken in two | a type of fish with no bones | a small muscle that moves the ear | *a piece of cartilage material, especially in the knee
